"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:09,499\nRetrieval practice\n\n4\n00:00:12,299 --> 00:00:14,460\nTwo groups study the same passage.\n\n5\n00:00:14,773 --> 00:00:16,868\nOne group reads it four times.\n\n6\n00:00:17,089 --> 00:00:18,443\nThe other reads it once,\n\n7\n00:00:18,488 --> 00:00:21,876\nthen tries three times to write down everything they remember,\n\n8\n00:00:21,896 --> 00:00:22,987\nwith no feedback.\n\n9\n00:00:23,513 --> 00:00:25,693\nFive minutes later, the rereaders win,\n\n10\n00:00:25,791 --> 00:00:27,722\neighty-three percent to seventy-one.\n\n11\n00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:29,825\nOne week later, it flips.\n\n12\n00:00:30,169 --> 00:00:32,285\nSixty-one percent for the testers,\n\n13\n00:00:32,384 --> 00:00:34,102\nforty for the rereaders.\n\n14\n00:00:34,272 --> 00:00:36,015\nRereading feels like learning,\n\n15\n00:00:36,035 --> 00:00:38,196\nbecause the text becomes familiar.\n\n16\n00:00:38,623 --> 00:00:40,946\nThe rereaders were the more confident group.\n\n17\n00:00:41,254 --> 00:00:44,381\nRetrieval feels harder, because you notice what's missing.\n\n18\n00:00:44,726 --> 00:00:46,963\nBut each time you pull a memory out,\n\n19\n00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,927\nyou strengthen the route back to it.\n\n20\n00:00:49,097 --> 00:00:51,320\nThis is not a lab curiosity.\n\n21\n00:00:51,806 --> 00:00:54,739\nA meta-analysis of two hundred seventy-two effects,\n\n22\n00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:57,018\nfrom one hundred eighteen articles,\n\n23\n00:00:57,299 --> 00:00:59,690\nfound practice tests beat restudying,\n\n24\n00:00:59,865 --> 00:01:02,076\nin classrooms as well as labs.\n\n25\n00:01:02,497 --> 00:01:03,695\nAnd in another study,\n\n26\n00:01:03,908 --> 00:01:06,335\nretrieval beat building concept maps,\n\n27\n00:01:06,602 --> 00:01:09,481\neven on questions that required inference.\n\n28\n00:01:09,651 --> 00:01:11,429\nYet students rarely choose it.\n\n29\n00:01:11,781 --> 00:01:15,845\nIn one survey, eighty-four percent of students reread their notes.\n\n30\n00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:18,785\nOnly eleven percent quizzed themselves.\n\n31\n00:01:18,955 --> 00:01:20,066\nTwo cautions.\n\n32\n00:01:20,388 --> 00:01:23,574\nFeedback matters, especially after wrong answers,\n\n33\n00:01:23,576 --> 00:01:24,844\nso errors don't stick.\n\n34\n00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:26,495\nAnd keep the stakes low.\n\n35\n00:01:26,755 --> 00:01:28,057\nThe point is practice,\n\n36\n00:01:28,077 --> 00:01:28,943\nnot grading.\n\n37\n00:01:29,113 --> 00:01:29,865\nFor teaching.\n\n38\n00:01:30,284 --> 00:01:34,316\nOpen class with two or three questions on last week's material.\n\n39\n00:01:34,701 --> 00:01:39,184\nAsk students to write down everything they remember before they look at their notes.\n\n40\n00:01:39,544 --> 00:01:43,441\nSwap some review lectures for practice problems and quick quizzes.\n\n41\n00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:45,182\nGive feedback fast.\n\n42\n00:01:45,453 --> 00:01:47,625\nAnd tell students why it feels hard.\n\n43\n00:01:47,795 --> 00:01:50,469\nPutting knowledge in is only half of learning.\n\n44\n00:01:50,873 --> 00:01:53,161\nPulling it back out is what makes it stay.\n\n"
